🔍 Step 1: Check the Volume Mapping
Verify that the container's volume /usr/local/apache2/htdocs is correctly mapped to the host's /var/www/html.

Check the container's volume configuration:

bash
docker inspect nautilus --format '{{json .Mounts}}' | python -m json.tool
Or for a simpler view:

bash
docker inspect nautilus | grep -A 10 "Mounts"
Expected output should show the mapping:

json
[
    {
        "Type": "bind",
        "Source": "/var/www/html",
        "Destination": "/usr/local/apache2/htdocs",
        "Mode": "",
        "RW": true,
        "Propagation": "rprivate"
    }
]
Key things to verify:

Source should be /var/www/html (host path)

Destination should be /usr/local/apache2/htdocs (container path)

RW: true indicates read-write access

You can also use docker inspect with a cleaner format:

bash
docker inspect -f '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{println}}{{end}}' nautilus
Expected output:

text
/var/www/html -> /usr/local/apache2/htdocs
If the volume is NOT mapped correctly, you would need to recreate the container with the proper mapping:

bash
docker rm -f nautilus
docker run -d --name nautilus -p 8085:80 -v /var/www/html:/usr/local/apache2/htdocs httpd
🔍 Step 2: Verify Website Accessibility on Host Port 8085
Check the port mapping:

bash
docker port nautilus
Expected output:

text
80/tcp -> 0.0.0.0:8085
Or check with docker ps:

bash
docker ps --filter "name=nautilus" --format "table {{.Names}}\t{{.Ports}}"
Expected output:

text
NAMES      PORTS
nautilus   0.0.0.0:8085->80/tcp
Check if the container is running:

bash
docker ps | grep nautilus
If the container is not running, start it:

bash
docker start nautilus
🔍 Step 3: Test with curl
Run the curl command on App Server 1:

bash
curl http://localhost:8085/
Expected output should be the HTML content of the static website, for example:

html
<html>
<body>
<h1>Welcome to the Nautilus Project</h1>
</body>
</html>
If you get a connection refused error, check:

Container is running: docker ps | grep nautilus

Port mapping is correct: docker port nautilus

No firewall blocking: systemctl status firewalld (or check iptables -L)

The web server inside the container is running:

bash
docker exec nautilus ps aux | grep httpd
Verbose curl for debugging:

bash
curl -v http://localhost:8085/
You can also check the HTTP status code:

bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:8085/
Expected output:

text
200
